Based on the repro and timing‐heatmaps, what’s happening is that by tying “waitForFCP” directly to `passConfig.recordTrace`, our default pass (which now records a trace by default) will stall indefinitely whenever a page never fires FCP—or only after the full 60 s protocol timeout does it finally abort. We only really want to waitForFCP on the *performance* pass, not on the default pass.

In practice the simplest fix is to keep `recordTrace` controlling whether we beginTracing, but gate the `waitForFCP` flag so it remains *false* on the “defaultPass” pass. That way, if the page never paints, we still proceed to endTrace (and surface a metric timeout) rather than hanging the entire pass for 60 s.

